# DESCRIPTION
# Base-5 multiplication problems to help with the understanding of the standard
# multiplicaiton algorithm.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Arithmetic')
## DBchapter('Other Number Systems')
## DBsection('Base 5 Numbers')
## KEYWORDS('base 5', 'multiplication')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextBaseN.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');


Context('BaseN')->setBase(5);

# In this context Real converts a decimal to a base-5 number.
$a1 = Real(random(5,24));
$b1 = Real(random(2,5));
$prod1 = $a1*$b1; 

$a2 = Real(random(5,24));
$b2 = Real(random(6,24));
$prod2 = $a2*$b2; 

$a3 = Real(random(5,124));
$b3 = Real(random(5,24));
$prod3 = $a3*$b3; 


# Don't allow students to enter arithmetic operations.
Context('LimitedBaseN')->setBase(5);
$ans1 = Real($prod1->value);
$ans2 = Real($prod2->value);
$ans3 = Real($prod3->value);

BEGIN_PGML

Each of the follow are multiplication problems in base-5.  Find the solution of each
without converting to decimal.  You may want to consider the base-5 multiplication table.

a) [`[$a1]_5 \times [$b1]_5`]=[________]*{$ans1}

b) [`[$a2]_5 \times [$b2]_5`]=[________]*{$ans2}

c) [`[$a3]_5 \times [$b3]_5`]=[________]*{$ans3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Recall that the base-5 multiplication table is

[#
[. [`\times`] .]{headerrow => 1} [. 1 .] [. 2 .] [. 3 .] [. 4 .]*
[. 1 .] [. 1 .] [. 2 .] [. 3 .] [. 4 .]*
[. 2 .] [. 2 .] [. 4 .] [. 11 .] [. 13 .]*
[. 3 .] [. 3 .] [. 11 .] [. 14 .] [. 22 .] *
[. 4 .] [. 4 .] [. 13 .] [. 22 .] [. 31 .]*
#]{align => 'r|rrrr'}

Then use the column algorithm to find the products using the table above.

END_PGML_HINT
@d1 = split //, "$a1";
@d2a = split //, "$a2";
@d2b = split //, "$b2"; 
@d3a = split //, "$a3";
@d3b = split //, "$b3";

Context('BaseN')->setBase(5);
$c1a = Compute("$b1 * $d1[0]");
$c1b = Compute("$b1 * $d1[1]");

$c2a = Compute("$d2a[0]*$d2b[0]");
$c2b = Compute("$d2a[0]*$d2b[1]");
$c2c = Compute("$d2a[1]*$d2b[0]");
$c2d = Compute("$d2a[1]*$d2b[1]");

$c3a = Compute("$d3a[0]*$d3b[0]");
$c3b = Compute("$d3a[0]*$d3b[1]");
$c3c = Compute("$d3a[1]*$d3b[0]");
$c3d = Compute("$d3a[1]*$d3b[1]");
$c3e = Compute("$d3a[2]*$d3b[0]");
$c3f = Compute("$d3a[2]*$d3b[1]");



BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For all of these operations, they are base-5. Although these don't explicitly use the column
algorithm, this is equivalent to the column algorithm

a) [`\;`]

    [```
    \begin{aligned}
    [$a1] \times [$b1] & = ([$d1[0]]0 + [$d1[1]]) \times [$b1] \\
    & = [$d1[0]]0 \times [$b1] + [$d1[1]] \times [$b1] \\
    & = [$c1a]0 + [$c1b] \\
    & = [$ans1]
    \end{aligned}
    ```]

b) [`\;`]

    [```
    \begin{aligned}
    [$a2] \times [$b2] & = ([$d2a[0]]0 + [$d2a[1]]) \times ([$d2b[0]]0 + [$d2b[1]]) \\
    & =  ([$d2a[0]]0 \times [$d2b[0]]0) + ([$d2a[0]]0 \times [$d2b[1]]) + ([$d2a[1]] \times [$d2b[0]]0) + ([$d2a[1]] \times [$d2b[1]])\\
    & = [$c2a]00 + [$c2b]0 + [$c2c]0 + [$c2d] \\
    & = [$ans2]
    \end{aligned}
    ```]

c) [`\;`]

[```
\begin{aligned}
[$a3] \times [$b3] & = ([$d3a[0]]00 + [$d3a[1]]0 + [$d3a[2]]) \times ([$d3b[0]]0 + [$d3b[1]]) \\
& =  ([$d3a[0]]00 \times [$d3b[0]]0) + ([$d3a[0]]00 \times [$d3b[1]]) + ([$d3a[1]]0 \times [$d3b[0]]0) + ([$d3a[1]]0 \times [$d3b[1]]) + ([$d3a[2]] \times [$d3b[0]]0) + ([$d3a[2]] \times [$d3b[1]])\\
& = [$c3a]000 + [$c3b]00 + [$c3c]00 + [$c3d]0 + [$c3e]0 + [$c3f] \\
& = [$ans3]
\end{aligned}
```]


END_PGML_SOLUTION



ENDDOCUMENT();
